from curriculum_library import dossier

dossier(61,'限时阅读：日常服务','A household service agreement',
'Starting the service~A home internet provider activates service after an engineer confirms the line is working. The billing period starts on activation, not on the date the customer first requests installation.||Appointment access~An adult must be present during the agreed installation window. If the engineer cannot gain access, a further visit may incur a charge. A key left with an unapproved neighbor does not meet the stated requirement.||Advertised speeds~The package describes an expected range rather than guaranteeing one speed at every moment. Equipment, connection method and local conditions can affect performance. Customers should follow the provider\'s testing instructions when reporting a fault.||Ending the agreement~The minimum contract period and early-exit conditions appear in the confirmation. Moving home does not automatically end all payment obligations; customers must contact the provider to discuss available options.',
'Reporting a fault~When my connection repeatedly failed, I recorded the times and whether the problem affected several devices. This helped distinguish a possible line problem from a fault with one laptop.||Following instructions~Support asked me to restart the equipment and test a wired connection. I noted the results rather than saying only that I had tried everything. Specific information reduced repeated questions.||A temporary solution~The provider offered a temporary mobile connection while arranging a visit. It had a data limit, so I checked what activities it could realistically support.||Keeping a record~I saved the fault reference and appointment details. When the first visit was postponed, I could identify the original commitment and ask about the applicable compensation policy.',
'The fault report~Customer: The connection drops every evening around seven. Adviser: Does it affect all devices? Customer: Yes, including the computer connected by cable.||A test~Adviser: Please note the router light when it happens. We have a line test available tonight, but it may briefly interrupt service.||An appointment~Customer: Can an engineer visit tomorrow? Adviser: The first available slot is Thursday afternoon. I can add you to the cancellation list.||Confirmation~Adviser: The cancellation list is not a confirmed earlier appointment. Keep Thursday unless we contact you with a replacement.',
'A parcel locker service~Parcels remain in the locker for three days after the collection message. After that period, they return to the depot.||Identification~Customers need the collection code. The order confirmation number is different and will not open the locker.||A damaged parcel~Visible damage should be photographed before opening where practical, then reported to the seller. Locker staff cannot authorize every seller\'s refund.||A size limit~Oversized parcels go directly to the depot. Choosing locker delivery does not override the published size restrictions.')

dossier(62,'限时阅读：工作文件','A travel-expense policy',
'Prior approval~Employees need manager approval before booking overnight work travel. Approval confirms the business purpose and budget; it does not remove the requirement to keep receipts.||Eligible transport~The company reimburses reasonable public transport fares. Taxis require an explanation when a suitable public option exists. Personal convenience alone may not satisfy the policy.||Meals and limits~Meal claims are subject to a daily limit. Unused allowance is not a cash payment, and alcohol is excluded. A restaurant receipt should identify what was purchased rather than show only a card total.||Submitting claims~Claims should be submitted within thirty days of the trip. Missing receipts require a written explanation and additional review. Reimbursement is not guaranteed simply because an expense was incurred during travel.',
'A claim returned~My expense claim was returned because I attached a bank statement instead of the hotel invoice. The statement showed payment but did not identify the dates, room charge or included meals.||Correcting the evidence~I requested a detailed invoice from the hotel. It showed that breakfast had already been included, so I removed a duplicate breakfast claim from my form.||Explaining an exception~I also explained a taxi journey taken after the final train was cancelled. I attached the cancellation notice rather than assuming the reviewer would know what happened.||Learning the process~The claim was approved after correction. The experience taught me to collect the required documents during the trip, when obtaining them is easier than several weeks later.',
'A policy enquiry~Employee: Can I claim the taxi from the station? Manager: Why was public transport unsuitable? Employee: The final connecting bus was cancelled.||Evidence~Manager: Include the cancellation notice and taxi receipt. The explanation matters because taxis are not routinely approved for that route.||A hotel issue~Employee: The hotel invoice includes breakfast. Manager: Then do not claim breakfast again as a separate meal.||The deadline~Manager: Submit within thirty days. If a receipt is delayed, tell finance before the deadline rather than waiting silently.',
'A remote-work equipment policy~Employees may request approved equipment needed for their role. Purchases made privately before approval may not be reimbursed.||Ownership~Equipment bought by the company remains company property and must be returned when requested or when employment ends.||Repairs~Faults should be reported to the support team. Employees should not arrange paid repairs without authorization.||Personal use~Limited personal use is permitted under the acceptable-use policy, but this does not transfer ownership or remove security requirements.')

dossier(63,'限时阅读：长文论证','Why a pilot can mislead',
'The appeal of a pilot~A small trial lets an organization test an idea before committing substantial resources. It can reveal practical problems cheaply. However, a successful pilot does not automatically establish that the same results will occur at full scale.||Unusual support~Pilot teams may receive extra attention, experienced staff and rapid technical help. Those conditions can make performance better than ordinary operation. Scaling up requires asking whether the same support can be maintained.||Selected participants~Early volunteers may be especially motivated or confident. Later users can have different needs. An evaluation should consider who did not participate as well as the experience of those who did.||A useful conclusion~The strongest pilot reports identify conditions under which the approach worked and problems still unresolved. They guide the next decision rather than presenting a limited experiment as universal proof.',
'A delivery trial~A shop tested same-day delivery within a small central area. Orders were light and two experienced drivers handled them. Almost every delivery arrived on time.||Expansion changes the task~The proposed expansion included distant addresses, peak-hour traffic and heavier order volumes. The original success rate could not be applied unchanged without checking these new conditions.||A staged response~The manager added one district at a time and tracked delays by route. This allowed the team to adjust capacity before promising the service everywhere.||An honest advertisement~The website listed eligible areas and order cut-off times. It did not imply that every customer could obtain same-day delivery regardless of location or demand.',
'Evaluating a trial~Researcher: The pilot had a ninety-five percent completion rate. Manager: Were participants representative of all staff?||A limitation~Researcher: Most volunteered and already used similar software. We should include less experienced users in the next stage.||Resources~Manager: Can we provide the same individual support to everyone? Researcher: Not with the current staffing level. We need clearer instructions and a realistic support budget.||Conclusion~Manager: Report the positive result with those limits. We have evidence to continue testing, not yet to guarantee the same outcome across the organization.',
'A school breakfast trial~One school offered free breakfast for a month with help from parent volunteers. Attendance increased during the trial.||Additional support~A local business supplied food without charge. The school will need a funding source if the scheme continues after the donation ends.||A missing comparison~The report does not compare attendance with similar schools. It cannot establish how much of the change was caused by breakfast alone.||Next steps~The school will examine costs, staffing and family feedback before deciding whether to extend the service.')

dossier(64,'限时听力：生活事务','A booking with several conditions',
'Room capacity~A community hall accepts up to sixty seated guests, but only forty when tables are required. The advertised maximum is not suitable for every seating arrangement.||Included time~Hire periods include setup and cleaning. A booking ending at six requires the room to be clear by six, not merely the event to have finished.||Equipment~A projector is available for an additional charge and must be reserved. The presence of a screen in the room does not mean projection equipment is automatically included.||Deposits~A security deposit is returned after inspection, provided the conditions are met. It is separate from the hire fee and should not be confused with an advance payment reducing that fee.',
'An organizer\'s checklist~I first confirmed how many guests would sit at tables. The hall\'s headline capacity was higher than the number suitable for our meal.||Allowing enough time~We booked an extra half-hour before the event for setting up and another afterwards for cleaning. Otherwise, volunteers would have needed to arrive before our authorized access began.||Checking equipment~We tested our laptop with the projector in advance. Having the correct cable was our responsibility under the hire agreement.||Confirming responsibilities~One person kept the contact numbers, booking confirmation and inspection checklist. This reduced confusion when different volunteers arrived at different times.',
'The enquiry~Caller: We expect fifty people for a meal. Adviser: With tables, this hall holds forty. The larger room next door holds seventy.||A revised choice~Caller: Let us use the larger room from two until six. Adviser: Does that include setting up and clearing away? Caller: We will need access from one thirty.||Equipment~Adviser: Do you need the projector? Caller: No, only the sound system. Adviser: That is included in the larger room\'s fee.||Final details~Adviser: I will quote for one thirty to six thirty, allowing cleanup. Please check those times before paying the deposit.',
'A sports booking~A court booking covers fifty-five minutes of play and five minutes for changeover. Players should leave equipment clear for the next group.||Footwear~Indoor shoes with non-marking soles are required. Clean outdoor shoes are not automatically suitable.||Cancellations~Bookings cancelled more than twelve hours ahead receive account credit. Cash refunds follow a separate policy.||Late arrival~Arriving late does not extend the booking. Staff may release an unused court after the grace period stated in the confirmation.')

dossier(65,'限时听力：讨论与讲解','Choosing a research question',
'An overlarge topic~A student group planned to investigate whether technology improves education. Their tutor explained that the question was too broad for a short project with limited time and participants.||Narrowing the focus~They chose to examine whether reminder messages helped classmates submit weekly practice tasks. This question defined an intervention, a group and an observable outcome.||Avoiding overclaiming~Submission did not reveal the quality of practice or long-term learning. The group therefore planned to describe participation rather than claim that reminders improved examination ability.||Considering ethics~Participants were told what information would be recorded and could decline. The group used anonymous totals in its presentation instead of displaying classmates\' names and individual performance.',
'A methods note~The group compared two weeks before reminders with two weeks after. This was easy to organize, but deadlines and workload might also differ across the periods.||An improvement~They recorded major timetable changes and asked participants whether reminders were useful or annoying. These comments provided context but were not treated as objective proof of improved learning.||Reporting a result~Submissions increased after reminders began. The group reported the change and its limitations, explaining that the design could not isolate every possible cause.||A future study~A longer project could compare similar groups or alternate reminder periods. The proposed improvement followed from a specific weakness in the original design rather than a vague call for more research.',
'The initial idea~Student A: Let us prove that technology makes learning better. Tutor: What can you actually measure in three weeks?||Narrowing~Student B: We could count whether reminders increase homework submissions. Tutor: That is more manageable, but submission is not the same as learning quality.||A design concern~Student A: Exams start in the final week. Tutor: Then workload changes may affect your result. Record that limitation.||A reporting rule~Tutor: Present anonymous totals and explain what your evidence supports. Do not claim more than the project can show.',
'A workplace survey~An office surveys staff about a new booking system. Responses are anonymous and participation is voluntary.||Scope~The questions concern ease of booking, not overall job satisfaction. Results should not be described as a measure of employee happiness.||Timing~The survey runs during the first week of use, when unfamiliarity may affect responses. A later survey is planned.||Publication~The report will summarize themes and totals. Individual written comments will be checked to avoid identifying respondents before publication.')

dossier(66,'限时写作：正式书信','Requesting action with evidence',
'A repeated fault~A tenant reported a faulty front-door lock twice. Temporary adjustments worked briefly, but the lock again failed to close properly. The concern involved building access and security, not merely appearance.||Relevant history~The tenant kept dates, repair references and a description of what happened after each visit. This record helped distinguish a recurring fault from a new unrelated complaint.||A proportionate request~She asked for a full inspection and a written plan, including an interim measure if a replacement could not be fitted promptly. She did not claim technical knowledge she did not possess.||Clear availability~The letter offered two access periods and asked the landlord to confirm one. A specific request and practical information made it easier for the recipient to act.',
'A useful opening~I am writing again about the front-door lock reported on 3 and 11 February. Although it was adjusted after both visits, it is no longer closing reliably.||Describing the consequence~Residents sometimes find the door unsecured. I cannot determine the mechanical cause, but the repeated failure suggests that another temporary adjustment may not be sufficient.||Requesting action~Please arrange a full inspection and explain whether the lock needs replacement. If this cannot happen promptly, please tell residents what interim measure will maintain secure access.||Closing practically~I can provide access on Tuesday morning or Thursday afternoon. Please confirm the appointment and the person responsible for updating us if arrangements change.',
'A repair update~Agent: The contractor can inspect on Thursday, but replacement parts may take a week. Tenant: What happens if the lock cannot be secured meanwhile?||An interim measure~Agent: The contractor will assess that during the visit and report any urgent requirement immediately. I cannot promise a temporary solution before inspection.||Access~Tenant: I am available Thursday after one. Agent: The slot is one to four. Please keep your phone available for the arrival call.||Written record~Tenant: Please send the reference and agreed window by email. Agent: I will include the previous repair references as well.',
'A public-lighting report~Residents can report a failed streetlight using the pole number or an exact location. General descriptions may delay identification.||Urgent hazards~Exposed wiring should be reported through the emergency number. Residents should not touch the equipment or attempt repairs.||Routine timescales~Ordinary bulb faults are scheduled according to the maintenance timetable. A report acknowledgement is not confirmation that repair has been completed.||Updates~If further information is needed, the maintenance team contacts the reporter using the details supplied with the report.')

dossier(67,'限时写作：观点展开','From a claim to an argument',
'An unsupported claim~A paragraph says public libraries are important but gives no explanation. The statement may be reasonable, yet the reader still needs to understand what benefit is meant and why it matters.||A causal explanation~Libraries provide access to information and study space for people whose homes lack suitable facilities. This can make independent learning more practical. The explanation connects the service to a consequence.||A relevant example~A worker sharing a crowded apartment may use a library to complete a training course. The example illustrates the mechanism without pretending to prove how every library user behaves.||A useful qualification~Libraries need appropriate opening hours and resources to provide that benefit. Recognizing conditions strengthens the argument by making it more precise, rather than weakening it through uncertainty alone.',
'A competing argument~Some people argue that online resources make physical libraries unnecessary. Digital access can indeed reduce the need to travel for many kinds of information.||Examining an assumption~This argument assumes reliable internet, suitable devices and a quiet place to work. Those conditions are not equally available to everyone.||A possible synthesis~Libraries can combine digital resources with physical space and assistance. The choice is not necessarily between preserving every old practice and closing buildings entirely.||A consistent position~A writer supporting that combined role should explain which services deserve priority. A conclusion that suddenly demands unlimited funding would go beyond the argument developed in the body.',
'A writing conference~Tutor: Your paragraph says online learning is convenient. Convenient in what way? Learner: People can study without travelling.||A consequence~Tutor: Why does that matter? Learner: It saves time and can help people with limited transport access.||A qualification~Tutor: Does it help everyone equally? Learner: No. A person still needs internet access and a suitable device.||A revision~Tutor: Add that condition and one concrete example. You do not need three more broad claims before explaining the first one.',
'A policy claim~A proposal says public parks improve wellbeing. To evaluate it, readers need to know how residents can actually use the spaces.||A mechanism~Safe paths and seating may encourage short walks and social contact. These features connect the park with potential benefits.||A limitation~Poor maintenance or difficult access can reduce use. The presence of green space on a map does not guarantee an accessible service.||An appropriate conclusion~Investment should consider design, location and upkeep, not only the total area labelled as parkland.')

dossier(68,'口语：比较与抽象话题','Moving from experience to ideas',
'Start with a distinction~A question about whether life is easier now can invite an overly broad answer. Distinguishing communication, work and daily services gives the speaker a clearer way to compare periods.||Explain a relationship~Technology may make information easier to obtain while increasing the amount people must evaluate. An answer can recognize both effects without becoming indecisive.||Use experience carefully~A personal example makes an idea concrete, but one experience does not prove a general trend. Phrases indicating scope help separate observation from universal claims.||Respond to challenge~An examiner or practice partner may ask about exceptions. This is an opportunity to refine the answer, not necessarily a signal that the original response was wrong.',
'A comparison example~When my parents planned a journey, they often relied on printed timetables. I can check live travel information, which makes changes easier to handle.||A remaining difficulty~The information is not always accurate, and a phone may lose its connection. Convenience therefore depends partly on infrastructure and the reliability of the service.||A broader point~People need both access to information and the ability to judge it. Simply providing more data does not remove the need for practical decisions.||A flexible conclusion~I would say many routine tasks are easier, but some choices are more complicated. Whether life feels easier also depends on a person\'s skills and circumstances.',
'A comparison question~Coach: How has shopping changed in your lifetime? Learner: Online ordering gives people more choice without travelling.||A challenge~Coach: Is that always better? Learner: Not necessarily. It can be difficult to judge quality, and returns take time.||A broader question~Coach: What might local shops offer that websites cannot? Learner: Immediate advice and the chance to examine an item, though this depends on the shop.||A final distinction~Coach: Would the answer differ for groceries and furniture? Learner: Yes. Size, freshness and delivery needs create different considerations.',
'A discussion activity~Participants compare learning alone with learning in a group. They must explain both a benefit and a limitation of each arrangement.||Evidence~Examples may come from personal experience, but speakers should identify when they are describing only their own circumstances.||Interaction~Partners ask how the answer changes for beginners or advanced learners. The follow-up tests flexibility rather than memorization.||Feedback~Listeners comment on clarity, development and responses to questions. Accent imitation is not the purpose of the activity.')

dossier(69,'错题：定位根因','Why an answer went wrong',
'Different causes~Two wrong answers may result from different problems. One learner misses a paraphrase, while another understands it but overlooks a negative word. Repeating the same general advice would not address both causes.||A useful error record~A record includes the original answer, correct evidence and reason for the mistake. Labels such as vocabulary, condition, reference and timing make recurring patterns easier to see.||Choosing the next task~A learner missing conditions needs practice with exceptions and limits. A learner running out of time may need passage navigation and task selection practice. More random questions are not always the best response.||Checking transfer~The correction is tested on a new item after a delay. Immediately repeating the original answer mainly shows that the solution is still in short-term memory.',
'An example error~I chose a service because the description said collection was available. I overlooked the condition limiting collection to addresses within the district.||The actual cause~The vocabulary was familiar. My mistake was stopping after finding a matching feature instead of checking the full condition. Learning another list of words would not directly solve it.||A targeted correction~I underlined limiting expressions in three new notices and explained who was excluded from each offer. This trained the step I had skipped.||A later check~A week later I answered a different matching task without hints. I recorded whether I checked every requirement before choosing, not only whether the final answer happened to be correct.',
'Reviewing an error~Coach: Why did you select Tuesday? Learner: It was the first date I heard. The speaker later changed it to Thursday.||Identifying the pattern~Coach: Was the word Thursday unfamiliar? Learner: No. I stopped checking after writing the first answer.||A better practice~Coach: Listen to short exchanges containing corrections and label the final arrangement. Then try an unfamiliar example.||Keeping perspective~Coach: One corrected item is progress, but look for the pattern across later tasks before deciding the problem is resolved.',
'A spelling record~A learner repeatedly omits the final letter in plural answers. The words are understood, but the written responses are inaccurate.||A targeted exercise~The learner listens for quantity cues and checks whether the answer requires singular or plural form. This is more specific than repeating a whole listening section.||A timing issue~Another learner makes errors only when transferring answers quickly. Practising the relevant test format is important because transfer arrangements can differ.||A follow-up~Both learners review unfamiliar items later. Their improvement is judged against the original error pattern rather than a single high practice score.')

dossier(70,'阶段检查：证据汇总','What a progress report should show',
'Separate records~A useful report distinguishes first attempts, assisted work and revisions. Combining them into one average can hide how independently the learner performed. Each record answers a different question.||Comparable conditions~Two scores are easier to compare when the tasks, timing and support are broadly similar. A higher result on a much shorter or previously seen task may not indicate equivalent progress.||Four different skills~Strong reading does not automatically establish strong speaking. The report keeps listening, reading, writing and speaking evidence visible rather than allowing one score to represent everything.||A cautious forecast~The report can identify promising progress and remaining gaps. It cannot guarantee an examination result, especially when writing or speaking has only informal feedback or full-session endurance has not been tested.',
'A review example~A learner has completed two unfamiliar reading sections within the time limit. Both results are stronger than the initial diagnostic, and the error log shows fewer missed conditions.||A weaker evidence area~Speaking records consist mainly of prepared monologues. The learner has little evidence of responding to unexpected questions. This gap remains despite an impressive vocabulary notebook.||A revised priority~The next fortnight keeps reading maintenance brief and increases spontaneous speaking practice within the same daily budget. The adjustment replaces some new material rather than extending every session.||A reporting decision~The summary says reading evidence has improved and speaking needs further assessment. It does not announce overall readiness based on the strongest single skill.',
'A review conversation~Learner: My reading score improved, so can I book with confidence? Coach: It is encouraging, but what evidence do you have for the other three skills?||A missing part~Learner: My writing has only self-ratings. Coach: Obtain feedback on an unfamiliar timed task and retain the draft. Label any AI estimate clearly.||A schedule constraint~Learner: I cannot practise for several hours in one day. Coach: Continue separate skill checks, but note that continuous-test endurance remains untested.||Next action~Coach: Choose the weakest evidence area for the next week and reduce optional new work to make space.',
'A training dashboard~The dashboard shows completed exercises, time records and assessment sources separately. Completion percentages are not labelled as language proficiency.||Missing information~An incomplete four-skill record does not produce an overall band. A blank field remains missing rather than becoming zero.||Source quality~Official results, mock assessments and self-estimates carry different labels. The dashboard does not treat them as equally authoritative.||Updates~Users can correct a mistaken date or score. The latest result is selected by assessment date, not simply by the order in which records were entered.')
